Invariant: after r completed rounds, each distance is the minimum cost of a walk from s using at most r relevant edges, or None if no such walk exists. Each round reads only the previous distance array, so extending a walk adds exactly one edge; copying the array preserves shorter walks. The `dist[u] is None` guard skips unreachable sources before arithmetic.

Precisely, an improvement during round k establishes unboundedness. Without a negative cycle, deleting nonnegative cycles leaves a simple path with at most k − 1 edges, so round k cannot improve any distance. Thus an improvement implies a negative cycle in the relevant subgraph: it is reachable from s and can reach T, so repeating it gives arbitrarily negative s-to-T costs. Conversely, a reachable negative cycle prevents distances from reaching a fixed point, so it forces an improvement in round k. An unchanged round is a fixed point and safely returns the finite optimum.

When s == T, the empty walk makes the target reachable and gives cost zero. The answer is unbounded exactly when a negative cycle is reachable from s and can return to s; otherwise it is finite with cost zero.

