**Addition order and coverage.** Write ⊕ for round-to-nearest float addition. Lanes 0–15 first form adjacent pairs, then contiguous groups of four, eight, and sixteen. At each round the XOR partner holds the complementary, disjoint half of the group; the operand order always places the lower-index half first. Induction therefore shows that all sixteen lanes obtain the same balanced-tree partial sum S, with inputs 0–15 appearing exactly once.

In round zero, lanes 16 and 17 both form Q = x16 ⊕ x17; lane 18 retains x18. In round one, lanes 16 and 17 read lane 18, while lane 18 reads lane 16. All three form R = Q ⊕ x18. In rounds two and three they read themselves but perform no addition, so nothing is double-counted. The last shuffle exchanges S and R, and every caller returns S ⊕ R. These partial sums have disjoint coverage whose union is all nineteen inputs.

